            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nThis text is a simple introduction to the multiplication tables of zero and one. We recommend reading *Adding the same numbers* (<htcite0>) first, where you will learn that multiplying is a way of adding the same number many times.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d862242c-0538-4b18-8b32-4a84d4a5858e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nA table is like a map of answers. When we know the map, we do not need to count everything again. Today we will learn two very special tables: the table of zero (0) and the table of one (1).",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Before we begin, remember that multiplying is a fast way of adding the same number many times.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_zero",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying by Zero (0)\n\nZero means \"nothing.\" Imagine 4 plates and no cookies on any plate. How many cookies are there?",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 1: Plates with no cookies</b><br />Each plate has 0 cookies.<br /><br />4 plates × 0 cookies = 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 = 0<br />8 plates × 0 cookies = 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 = 0<br /><br />No matter how many plates we have, if every plate is empty, there are no cookies.</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "When we multiply a number by zero, the answer is always zero. This works for people, trees, cookies, or anything else.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Empty groups</b><br /><br />If every box is empty, can we find something inside it?<br /><br />That is why every multiplication by zero gives zero.</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "We can write this rule as a formula:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p class=\"center_notation\"><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>0</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>0</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 0</b>: Any number multiplied by zero is zero.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- The **result** (left side) is the product.\n- The **factors** are on the right side.\n- **N** can be any number.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_one",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying by One (1)\n\nNow let's learn what happens when we multiply by one (1).\n\n#### Counting on our fingers\n\nA simple way to understand multiplication by one is to count on your fingers. Use the arrows in Table 2 to see this in action:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"tawapukllay\" id=\"tblHandsCounting\"><tr><th>Left Hand</th><th>Right Hand</th><th>Arrows</th></tr> <tr id=\"tr0\"><td><img id=\"lefthand\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('lefthand', '0%')\" /></td><td><img id=\"righthand\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('righthand', '0%')\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white;\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-caret-up upArrowWithFA\" id=\"traineeUp0\"></i>  <i class=\"fa-solid fa-caret-down downArrowWithFA\"style=\"font-size:4em;color:#00f;\" id=\"traineeDown0\"></td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 2</b>: Examples of counting and multiplication by 1.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Multiplying a number by one means adding one repeatedly until you reach that number. For example, 3 × 1 means 1 + 1 + 1 = 3. The answer is always the same number (<htcite0>)",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7a9c733b-ca13-40f5-8849-19012540b028",
                     "text": "Ernst, Dana.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "We can write this rule as a formula:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Any number multiplied by one is the same number.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Look at Table 2: when the arrow points to 4, we have 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4, and 4 × 1 = 4.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text we learned two special rules:\n\n- Any number multiplied by zero gives zero: 0 = 0 × N.\n- Any number multiplied by one stays the same: N = 1 × N.\n\nTry these rules with numbers you know. You will see that they work every time.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
   "exercise_v2": [
      {
         "question": "If we have 3 empty boxes, is the total number of toys inside them zero?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "0 + 0 + 0 = 0, so 3 × 0 = 0."
      },
      {
         "question": "Does multiplying a number by one change the number?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "No",
         "additionalInfo": "Multiplying by one keeps the same number: 5 × 1 = 5."
      },
      {
         "question": "Is 7 × 0 equal to 7?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "No",
         "additionalInfo": "Any number multiplied by zero is zero: 7 × 0 = 0."
      }
   ],
